[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 986,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 951,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148630,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 951,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenosquamous carcinoma of the stomach is a gastric carcinoma composed of distinct adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) components, with either component accounting for &ge;&nbsp;25% of the tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 987,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 952,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148631,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 952,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>85600/3 Adenosquamous carcinoma, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 988,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 953,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148632,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 953,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2B72.Y &amp; XH7873 Adenosquamous carcinoma of stomach</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 989,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 954,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148633,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 954,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 990,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 955,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148634,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 955,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 991,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 956,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148635,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 956,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gastric adenosquamous carcinoma may occur in all areas of the stomach {28676632;39003328}.</p>\r\n<p>Distal extension from the more common adenosquamous carcinomas of the lower oesophagus should be excluded by correlation with available macroscopic information (endoscopic or gross evaluation).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 992,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 957,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148636,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 957,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical features of gastric adenosquamous carcinoma are similar to those of other types of gastric cancer. Most tumours present as large masses, with a mean size of 60&nbsp;mm {25889482;28676632;3942965}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 993,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 958,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148637,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 958,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gastric adenosquamous carcinoma is rare, accounting for &lt;&nbsp;0.4% of all gastric cancers {22788926;25567665}. Men are predominantly affected, with a mean age of 62&nbsp;years {3942965;25889482}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 994,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 959,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148638,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 959,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 995,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 960,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148639,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 960,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The definitive pathogenesis remains controversial. Tumours may originate from pluripotent stem cells or derive from squamous metaplasia in pre-existing gastric adenocarcinoma {25889482}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1002,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 967,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148640,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 967,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is similar to that of other gastric cancers.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 996,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 961,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148641,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 961,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gastric adenosquamous carcinoma shows distinct components of adenocarcinoma and SCC in variable proportions; either component should account for&ge;&nbsp;25% of the tumour {22788926}. The squamous cell component mirrors conventional oesophageal SCC (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/72/13\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma</em>). The presence of adenoid or microcystic changes within otherwise pure squamous cell carcinoma is insufficient for the diagnosis of gastric adenosquamous carcinoma.</p>\r\n<p>Mucin staining is useful for highlighting adenocarcinoma areas, and p63/p40 or high-molecular-weight keratin (34&beta;E12) and CK5/6 immunohistochemistry may confirm the presence and extent of the squamous cell component.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 997,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 962,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148642,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 962,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Peritoneal lavage cytology is used for gastric cancer staging and monitoring chemotherapy (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/72/27\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Gastric adenocarcinoma</em>).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 998,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 963,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148643,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 963,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Limited data are available (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/72/27\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Gastric adenocarcinoma</em>).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1001,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 966,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148644,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 966,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a carcinoma with both glandular and squamous cell components.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 999,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 964,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148645,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 964,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Spread of tumour to regional nodes and to liver, lung, or peritoneum is common {3942965}. Lymph node metastases are mostly from the adenocarcinoma component, but the squamous cell component or both components may be seen in the metastases {25889482;25567665;28676632}. For TNM staging, see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/72/27\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Gastric adenocarcinoma</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1000,
    "bookid": 72,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 965,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 148646,
        "bookId": 72,
        "chapterId": 29,
        "headingId": 965,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Because of the rarity of this entity, data on specific prognostic factors are limited and stage is an independent determinant of outcome {33380867}.</p>\r\n<p>Gastric adenosquamous carcinoma seems to behave more aggressively than gastric adenocarcinoma of the same stage {30788163;31737107}. An increased proportion of the squamous cell component has been associated with worse prognosis {32899039;39003328}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-06-09T11:26:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]